p://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"t equals p Superscript 3 n minus 1\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"n\"><mi>n</mi></math> is a constant, then substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"p Superscript 3 n minus 1\"><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"t\"><mi>t</mi></math> in the equation yields&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"p Superscript two thirds Baseline equals p Superscript nine sevenths left parenthesis 3 n minus 1 right parenthesis\"><msup><mi>p</mi><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></math>. It's given that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"p greater than 1\"><mi>p</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>; therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"two thirds equals nine sevenths left parenthesis 3 n minus 1 right parenthesis\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. Multiplying each side of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"21\"><mn>21</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 equals 27 left parenthesis 3 n minus 1 right parenthesis\"><mn>14</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. Distributing multiplication over subtraction yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"14 equals 81 n minus 27\"><mn>14</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>81</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>27</mn></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn></math> to each side of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"41 equals 81 n\"><mn>41</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>81</mn><mi>n</mi></math>. Dividing each side of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"81\"><mn>81</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 41 Over 81 EndFraction equals n\"><mfrac><mn>41</mn><mn>81</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></math>. Therefore, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"t equals p Superscript 3 n minus 1\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"n\"><mi>n</mi></math> is a constant, the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"n\"><mi>n</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 41 Over 81 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>41</mn><mn>81</mn></mfrac></math>. Note that 41/81, .5061, .5062, and 0.506 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>","externalid":"763bb625-3667-4ea6-af9f-195d80193bdb","correct_answer":[".5061",".5062","41/81"]},"createDate":1776362727992,"updateDate":1776362727992}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727992, 1776362727992),
    ($SATQ$568aaf27$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$032680-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables $SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="stimulus_reference " xmlns="http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1"><p class="standalone_statement style:1 "><span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_000_7b04575b.png" alt="Equation 1: x plus y equals 12. Equation 2: y equals x squared."></span></span></p></div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">If <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_001_7284e904.png" alt="the ordered pair x comma y"></span> is a solution to the system of equations above, which of the following is a possible value of <span class="italic">x</span>?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">2</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">3</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. Substituting <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_002_572bc813.png" alt="x squared "></span> from the second equation for <span class="italic">y</span> in the first equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_003_5a7e4d2d.png" alt="x plus x squared, equals 12"></span>. Subtracting 12 from both sides of this equation and rewriting the equation results in <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_004_01a7ab87.png" alt="x squared, plus x, minus 12, equals 0"></span>. Factoring the left-hand side of this equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_005_2a6667fa.png" alt="open parenthesis, x minus 3, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, x plus 4, close parenthesis, equals 0"></span>. Using the zero product property to solve for <span class="italic">x</span>, it follows that <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_006_01515e35.png" alt="x minus 3, equals 0 "></span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_007_13821c1e.png" alt="x plus 4, equals 0"></span>. Solving each equation for <span class="italic">x</span> yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_008_6ec6a793.png" alt="x equals 3 "></span> and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_009_e9fe8b6b.png" alt="x equals negative 4"></span>, respectively. Thus, two possible values of <span class="italic">x</span> are 3 and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_010_4d86fa3c.png" alt="negative 4"></span>. Of the choices given, 3 is the only possible value of <span class="italic">x</span>.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect. Substituting 0 for <span class="italic">x</span> in the first equation gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_011_3a0fc258.png" alt="0 plus y, equals 12"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_012_ddc551a4.png" alt="y equals 12"></span>; then, substituting 12 for <span class="italic">y</span> and 0 for <span class="italic">x</span> in the second equation gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_013_eb82400c.png" alt="12 equals 0 squared"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_014_09551047.png" alt="12 equals 0"></span>, which is false. Similarly, substituting 1 or 2 for <span class="italic">x </span>in the first equation yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_015_9cfe788b.png" alt="y equals 11 "></span> or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_016_a3218e2f.png" alt="y equals 10"></span>, respectively; then, substituting 11 or 10 for <span class="italic">y</span> in the second equation yields a false statement.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 17, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"568aaf27","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"032680-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"P","domain":"Advanced Math","skill_cd":"P.B.","skill_desc":"Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables ","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \" xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\"><p class=\"standalone_statement style:1 \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_000_7b04575b.png\" alt=\"Equation 1: x plus y equals 12. Equation 2: y equals x squared.\"></span></span></p></div>\n","stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">If <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_001_7284e904.png\" alt=\"the ordered pair x comma y\"></span> is a solution to the system of equations above, which of the following is a possible value of <span class=\"italic\">x</span>?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">2</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">3</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_002_572bc813.png\" alt=\"x squared \"></span> from the second equation for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> in the first equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_003_5a7e4d2d.png\" alt=\"x plus x squared, equals 12\"></span>. Subtracting 12 from both sides of this equation and rewriting the equation results in <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_004_01a7ab87.png\" alt=\"x squared, plus x, minus 12, equals 0\"></span>. Factoring the left-hand side of this equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_005_2a6667fa.png\" alt=\"open parenthesis, x minus 3, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, x plus 4, close parenthesis, equals 0\"></span>. Using the zero product property to solve for <span class=\"italic\">x</span>, it follows that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_006_01515e35.png\" alt=\"x minus 3, equals 0 \"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_007_13821c1e.png\" alt=\"x plus 4, equals 0\"></span>. Solving each equation for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_008_6ec6a793.png\" alt=\"x equals 3 \"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_009_e9fe8b6b.png\" alt=\"x equals negative 4\"></span>, respectively. Thus, two possible values of <span class=\"italic\">x</span> are 3 and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_010_4d86fa3c.png\" alt=\"negative 4\"></span>. Of the choices given, 3 is the only possible value of <span class=\"italic\">x</span>.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect. Substituting 0 for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> in the first equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_011_3a0fc258.png\" alt=\"0 plus y, equals 12\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_012_ddc551a4.png\" alt=\"y equals 12\"></span>; then, substituting 12 for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> and 0 for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> in the second equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_013_eb82400c.png\" alt=\"12 equals 0 squared\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_014_09551047.png\" alt=\"12 equals 0\"></span>, which is false. Similarly, substituting 1 or 2 for <span class=\"italic\">x </span>in the first equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_015_9cfe788b.png\" alt=\"y equals 11 \"></span> or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/568aaf27/img_016_a3218e2f.png\" alt=\"y equals 10\"></span>, respectively; then, substituting 11 or 10 for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> in the second equation yields a false statement.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":17,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959627,"pPcc":"SAT#P","questionId":"568aaf27","skill_cd":"P.B.","score_band_range_cd":2,"skill_desc":"Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables ","createDate":1691007959627,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Advanced Math","ibn":"032680-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"P","uId":"819e8084-a758-4a17-8699-8cda78e28751","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"032680-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \" xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\"><p class=\"standalone_statement style:1 \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Equation 1: x plus y equals 12. Equation 2: y equals x squared.\"></span></span></p></div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">If <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the ordered pair x comma y\"></span> is a solution to the system of equations above, which of the following is a possible value of <span class=\"italic\">x</span>?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">0</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">2</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">3</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"d","rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. Substituting <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x squared \"></span> from the second equation for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> in the first equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x plus x squared, equals 12\"></span>. Subtracting 12 from both sides of this equation and rewriting the equation results in <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x squared, plus x, minus 12, equals 0\"></span>. Factoring the left-hand side of this equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"open parenthesis, x minus 3, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, x plus 4, close parenthesis, equals 0\"></span>. Using the zero product property to solve for <span class=\"italic\">x</span>, it follows that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x minus 3, equals 0 \"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x plus 4, equals 0\"></span>. Solving each equation for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals 3 \"></span> and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals negative 4\"></span>, respectively. Thus, two possible values of <span class=\"italic\">x</span> are 3 and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 4\"></span>. Of the choices given, 3 is the only possible value of <span class=\"italic\">x</span>.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect. Substituting 0 for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> in the first equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"0 plus y, equals 12\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"y equals 12\"></span>; then, substituting 12 for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> and 0 for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> in the second equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"12 equals 0 squared\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"12 equals 0\"></span>, which is false. Similarly, substituting 1 or 2 for <span class=\"italic\">x </span>in the first equation yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"y equals 11 \"></span> or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"y equals 10\"></span>, respectively; then, substituting 11 or 10 for <span class=\"italic\">y</span> in the second equation yields a false statement.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959627,"updateDate":1691007959627}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959627, 1691007959627),
    ($SATQ$5805e747$SATQ$, $SATQ$891c7c48-227c-4992-acdb-e5e19b360217$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$66e19bbf-02ed-4d38-a94a-8bc12f0b4339$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Equivalent expressions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Which expression is equivalent to <math alttext="left parenthesis 7 x cubed plus 7 x right parenthesis minus left parenthesis 6 x cubed minus 3 x right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dcdd3a74-8f8c-467c-af10-87d0f0790c89","content":"<p><math alttext=\"x cubed plus 10 x\"><mrow>\n\t<msup>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msup>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>10</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"320e8d68-fe41-4973-a460-e0533e873ef5","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 13 x cubed plus 10 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t\t<mn>13</mn>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>10</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e1e525e5-e692-4db9-b611-25823d727f76","content":"<p><math alttext=\"minus 13 x cubed plus 4 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t\t<mn>13</mn>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"42b3972f-483a-4d10-acfc-2c55c9e17ecd","content":"<p><math alttext=\"x cubed plus 4 x\"><mrow>\n\t<msup>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t<mn>3</mn>\n\t</msup>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dcdd3a74-8f8c-467c-af10-87d0f0790c89"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. Applying the distributive property, the given expression can be written as <math alttext="7 x cubed plus 7 x minus 6 x cubed plus 3 x"><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></math>. Grouping like terms in this expression yields <math alttext="left parenthesis 7 x cubed minus 6 x cubed right parenthesis plus left parenthesis 7 x plus 3 x right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>. Combining like terms in this expression yields <math alttext="x cubed plus 10 x"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></math>.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
